 with June core PCE at 3.3% year-over-year and headline CPI at 3.5%, alongside Middle East supply shocks lifting energy prices, continues to anchor trader expectations for a potential 25-basis-point hike. The July 29 FOMC meeting left the federal funds rate at 3.50–3.75% on a 9-3 vote, with three dissents favoring an immediate increase, while the fresh July employment report showed an unexpected 23,000 nonfarm payroll decline and a 4.1% unemployment rate. CME FedWatch currently prices roughly even odds for a September move. The September 15–16 FOMC meeting, August PCE and CPI releases, and any further geopolitical developments remain the key near-term catalysts that could shift the market-implied rate path.","context_requires_regen":false,"context_updated_at":"2026-08-11T23:01:18.974Z"},"version":"v1"}]
